The family is viable only with a clear caveat: the paper proves worst-case NP-hardness for clique and polynomial recovery for sufficiently large random plants; it does not prove average-case hardness below that threshold. I’m using the standard \(G(N,\tfrac12)\) planted-clique model with \(k\) growing roughly as \(N^{0.45}\), where no polynomial-time recovery algorithm is known and the paper’s \(k=\Omega(\sqrt N)\) guarantee does not apply. The checker asks only for a size-\(k\) clique, never for an optimum or a proof of uniqueness.
